The code statements that lead to the bug are:
write_unlock_bh(&idev->lock);
ipv6_add_acaddr_hash(net, aca);
Reason:
The write_unlock_bh(&idev->lock) allows a concurrent ipv6_ac_destroy_dev() under RTNL to remove the aca from idev->ac_list and call ipv6_del_acaddr_hash() (which is a no-op because the aca is not yet in the global hash) and then free the aca. After that, ipv6_add_acaddr_hash() inserts the already-freed aca into the global hash, leading to a use-after-free when a later RCU reader dereferences the dangling node.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
ipv6_add_acaddr_hash(net, aca);
write_unlock_bh(&idev->lock);
spin_lock_bh(&acaddr_hash_lock);
spin_unlock_bh(&acaddr_hash_lock);
Reason:
Moving ipv6_add_acaddr_hash() inside the idev->lock section ensures that the insertion into the global hash is atomic with respect to the removal from idev->ac_list. A racing teardown either sees the aca in both lists before removal or misses it entirely. Changing acaddr_hash_lock to spin_lock_bh() avoids a softirq lock inversion because idev->lock is acquired in softirq context and acaddr_hash_lock is now nested under it.

